# SIM7600EI Cellular Gateway - Dashboard configuration
#
# Copy to packages/dashboard/.env.local (git-ignored) and restart the dashboard. Next.js reads .env files from THIS folder only,
# not from the repository root .env that the gateway uses.
#
# Hardware settings (COM ports, audio rate, driver) are never set here. They belong to the gateway host's own .env.

# ---- Server-side only: used by the dashboard SERVER for the SMS page and the status indicators ----------------------------
# These are NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables, so they are never sent to the browser.
#
# Same PC as the gateway (the normal case): you can leave all of these out. The dashboard server then falls back to the
# repository root .env (GATEWAY_API_KEY and PORT only) and contacts http://127.0.0.1:<PORT>.
#
# Dashboard on another computer (for example a Mac) than the gateway (the Windows PC with the modem): set both.
# GATEWAY_API_KEY=<the same value as GATEWAY_API_KEY in the gateway's .env>
# GATEWAY_INTERNAL_URL=http://192.168.1.24:4000
#
# The SMS relay only answers a page opened as http://localhost:3000 on the machine running the dashboard. To use it from another
# machine deliberately (the dashboard has no login of its own, so anyone who can open it could send SMS):
# DASHBOARD_SMS_ALLOW_REMOTE=true

# ---- Browser side (optional) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The live call controls talk to the gateway from the browser. Leave these unset and use the Settings page (/settings): the
# address and key entered there stay in this browser only. With no address saved, the browser uses the host the page was
# opened from, on port 4000. Do NOT put the real key in a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable: it would be built into the page for everyone
# who can load it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GATEWAY_URL=http://192.168.1.24:4000
